urself</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Name the three features that every definition of an agent shares.</li>\n<li>Which of the five patterns has no fixed end, and what does it therefore need?</li>\n<li>Give a loan example of routing.</li>\n<li>Why can an LLM not answer \"what is 48,317 x 29\" reliably on its own, and what fixes that?</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answers\"><summary>Show answers</summary><p> (1) An LLM, tools and some autonomy. (2) The evaluator-optimizer, because it loops; it needs a stopping rule and a turn limit. (3) Classify each message as a database question, a policy question or both, then send it down the matching path. (4) LLMs generate text and can get arithmetic wrong; a code interpreter or calculator tool computes it exactly.</p></details>\n<p><strong>Go deeper:</strong> Shaw Talebi, <a class=\"vlink\" href=\"#/watch/ZaY5_ScmiFE\">An Introduction to AI Agents</a> · Anthropic, <a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Building effective agents</a> · Yao et al., <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models</a> (the paper behind reason-then-act loops).</p>\n"}